 "api_error_status": null,
 "result": "This is a Flask-like framework named `tessel`. Order of calls:\n\n1. `Tessel.__call__` (`src/tessel/app.py:1618`) just delegates to `wsgi_entry`.\n2. `Tessel.wsgi_entry` (`src/tessel/app.py:1566`) is the real WSGI app.\n3. `Tessel.request_context` (`src/tessel/app.py:1501`) calls `AppContext.from_environ` (`src/tessel/ctx.py:340`) to build the context and request.\n4. `AppContext.push` (`src/tessel/ctx.py:416`):\n   - sets the context var;\n   - sends `appcontext_pushed`;\n   - opens the session with `_get_session` (`ctx.py:381`);\n   - runs `match_request` (`ctx.py:405`) for URL routing. Routing errors are stored on the request, not raised.\n5. `Tessel.run_full_request` (`src/tessel/app.py:992`):\n   - sends `request_started`;\n   - calls `preprocess_request` (`app.py:1366`), which runs the before-request handlers. A non-None return short-circuits the view.\n6. `Tessel.run_view` (`src/tessel/app.py:966`):\n   - raises any stored routing exception;\n   - handles automatic OPTIONS;\n   - looks up `view_functions[rule.endpoint]`;\n   - calls it through `ensure_sync` (`app.py:1065`) with `view_args`. This is where the view runs.\n\nIf an exception occurs along the way, `route_user_fault` handles it inside `run_full_request`, and `route_unhandled_fault` handles anything outside it in `wsgi_entry`.\n\nAfter the view, `finalize_request` (`app.py:1021`) builds the response, then `ctx.pop` tears down the context.",
